{"qid":"Q172107","names":["Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth"],"title":"Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth","endonym":{"native":"Regnum Poloniae Magnusque Ducatus Lithuaniae","lang":"la"},"capsule":"The Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth was created by the Union of Lublin on 1 July 1569, merging the Kingdom of Poland and the Grand Duchy of Lithuania into a single elective monarchy governed by a noble Sejm that operated under the principle of the liberum veto. At its greatest extent in the early seventeenth century it was among the largest states in Europe, stretching from the Baltic toward the Black Sea steppe, and under Stephen Báthory (r. 1576–1586) and Sigismund III it waged war against Muscovy, briefly occupying Moscow in 1610–1612. Chronic paralysis of the Sejm invited foreign interference, and three partitions by Russia, Prussia, and Austria in 1772, 1793, and 1795 erased the Commonwealth; the Kościuszko Uprising of 1794 was the final armed bid to reverse this dismemberment before the third partition extinguished the state.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territories in Poland, Lithuania, and Ukraine are documented through the Sejm records and cadastral surveys. The eastern and southern frontier with the Ottoman Empire and Muscovy was militarily contested and changed repeatedly; the Cossack Hetmanate zone after 1648 is particularly difficult to delimit precisely.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[],"britannica":"place/Polish-Lithuanian-Commonwealth","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth","rulers":[{"qid":"Q54058","name":"Sigismund II Augustus","start":1530,"end":1572,"image":"Cranach the Y